{"hash":"b9d59800588053a9","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":88.531,"said":"And now you stand before Shri Mallikarjuna. Remember the parents who would not leave their son, the princess with her jasmine, the Mother of the bees. Here, tradition says, Shiva and Parvati dwell side by side, as Mallikarjuna and Bhramaramba, out of love for their son and for all who come to them. Fold your hands. The second verse of the Dwadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram, traditionally attributed to Adi Shankaracharya, sings of this place: ‘Shrishailashringe vibudhatisange tuladritunge’pi muda vasantam, tam Arjunam Mallikapurvam ekam namami samsarasamudrasetum.’ ‘On the peak of Srishaila, where the gods gather, on that lofty mountain he dwells in joy: to that One, Arjuna with Mallika before his name, I bow, the bridge across the ocean of worldly life.’ Srisailam is the second Jyotirlinga of the Stotram. Pilgrims may visit the twelve Jyotirlingas in any order their journey allows; by tradition, the yatra begins at Somnath, the first, and is held complete with darshan at Grishneshwar, the twelfth, near Ellora. The lamps are being lit, one form after another, until a single flame remains. Let the Maha Mangala Harathi begin. Om Namah Shivaya.\n\nDid you know? Srisailam’s full temple name honours both: Sri Bhramaramba Mallikarjuna Swamy Varla Devasthanam.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1152},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1168,"end":1264}],"words":[[0.098,0.18,0,3],[0.29,0.204,4,7],[0.507,0.204,8,11],[0.723,0.421,12,17],[1.156,0.361,18,24],[1.528,0.24,25,29],[1.781,0.817,30,42],[3.51,0.493,44,52],[4.014,0.096,53,56],[4.123,0.481,57,64],[4.615,0.144,65,68],[4.772,0.156,69,74],[4.94,0.276,75,78],[5.228,0.228,79,84],[5.469,0.18,85,90],[5.661,0.421,91,94],[6.322,0.096,96,99],[6.43,0.517,100,108],[6.959,0.168,109,113],[7.139,0.12,114,117],[7.272,0.589,118,125],[8.053,0.12,127,130],[8.185,0.337,131,137],[8.534,0.132,138,140],[8.678,0.108,141,144],[8.798,0.457,145,149],[10.1